  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nConvection was developing across the Great Lakes region early\nthis afternoon, and will continue to track southeastward. The\nlatest mesoanalysis shows increasing diurnal instability across\nthe region, with ML CAPE ranging from 1000-2000 j/kg. Capping\nwas observed on the 12Z PIT sounding just under 700mb, which\nwill likely initially limit convective potential across the area\nearly this afternoon. Some erosion of the cap is expected\nthrough the day, though a warm layer remains around 700mb\nthrough early this evening. \n\nExpect scattered convection to develop across areas north of \nI-70 this afternoon as convective temperatures are reached, and \nas the cap slowly erodes. Additional showers and thunderstorms \ncould become focused along a lake breeze, and with outflow from \nconvection upstream. Some storms could contain damaging wind\ngusts, with increasing shear and dry mid level air. The latest\nday one outlook from the Storm Prediction Center maintained a\nSlight Risk for severe storms across NW PA into ern OH, with a\nMarginal Risk elsewhere. \n\nAdditional showers and thunderstorms are expected to cross the\nUpper Ohio Valley region tonight ahead of an approaching\nshortwave trough and surface cold front. A potential MCS could\nclip some of the Ohio counties later this evening as well. The\nmost favorable instability (1500-2500 j/kg ML CAPE), and shear\n(30-40kt) is expected to be across Ohio, with lesser values to\nthe east. This lines up well with the latest day one outlook\nfrom SPC. The severe potential should wane somewhat overnight,\nthrough there should be enough instability to maintain\nthunderstorms. Locally heavy rainfall will be possible if any\ntraining occurs, though the limited potential and uncertainty \nprecludes a flood watch at this time. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nExpect some ongoing convection and cloud cover Tuesday morning\nas the cold front/shortwave continue to approach. Increasing\ninstability near and SE of the cold front should result in a\nMarginal Risk of severe storms, mainly south of a DUJ-BTP-ZZV\nline. The extent of any morning convection and cloud cover will\nhave an impact on the amount of instability, and resulting\nsevere weather potential through the day. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms should begin to diminish by evening as\nthe front crosses and begins to exit the region, and as\ninstability decreases. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nThe shortwave trough is progged to deepen into an upper low\nacross the NE CONUS by mid week. Wrap around moisture and lift\nshould result in shower chances continuing east of I-77 on\nWednesday, decreasing further from W-E on Thursday as the low\ndrifts eastward. Shortwave ridging is then progged to move\nacross the Upper Ohio Valley region later Thursday through\nSaturday, with dry and seasonable weather expected. The next\nchance for showers and thunderstorms is expected Saturday night\nand Sunday with the next approaching shortwave trough.",
